WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.220
The first film scored each partner's side as a net.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.740
When both partners evolve, each resets the other's net.

3
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.120
How can you see that?

4
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:18.143
Take a bacterium and a phage, a virus that infects bacteria.

5
00:00:19.423 --> 00:00:22.763
Freeze samples of both every two weeks, to revive later.

6
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:26.693
Now cross them in a time-shift assay.

7
00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:32.213
Phages from one time meet hosts from the past, the same time and the future.

8
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:40.657
If neither partner's heritable traits changed, every pairing should give about the

9
00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:41.777
same infection rate.

10
00:00:43.037 --> 00:00:46.317
A repeatable difference means at least one partner changed.

11
00:00:47.577 --> 00:00:48.717
The shape hints at how.

12
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.440
First, an arms race.

13
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.920
Host resistance and phage infectivity both rise, in repeated rounds.

14
00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.423
Take one phage from the middle of the series.

15
00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:04.323
Test it on past, same-time and future hosts.

16
00:01:05.603 --> 00:01:06.883
Which does it infect best?

17
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.760
The past hosts. This phage has never met the resistance that later hosts evolved.

18
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.860
Infection falls from past hosts to future ones, and keeps falling as the gap grows.

19
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.320
Fluctuating selection works differently.

20
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.320
Whichever host type is common gets attacked most.

21
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.480
Rare types then gain, and the advantage keeps shifting.

22
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.990
Phages track the common host type, so they tend to do best on hosts of about their

23
00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:52.330
own time. As the gap grows, infection rises and falls, with no long-run trend.

24
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.950
So read the shape as evidence, not proof.

25
00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.050
A one-way trend points to escalation.

26
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.010
A wave with no long-run trend points to tracking.

27
00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:07.603
Evolution can also act within a population cycle.

28
00:02:09.143 --> 00:02:13.143
Picture a chemostat, a vessel with a steady inflow and a matching outflow.

29
00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:15.783
Rotifers graze on algae.

30
00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:22.067
In classical models, prey rise, predators multiply, prey crash, predators starve.

31
00:02:23.587 --> 00:02:26.647
Rotifer peaks trail algal peaks by about a quarter cycle.

32
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:31.247
That delay, as a fraction of the period, is the phase lag.

33
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.220
Takehito Yoshida and colleagues started the algae in two ways.

34
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.420
A clone is a set of genetically identical cells.

35
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.540
One clone cannot evolve by selection among clones.

36
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.060
Several clones can.

37
00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:51.603
One clone gave short cycles, close to the quarter lag.

38
00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:58.023
Several clones gave long cycles, with rotifer and algal peaks largely out of step.

39
00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:01.087
The algae carry their own balance.

40
00:03:02.407 --> 00:03:05.967
Defence against grazing costs them in nutrient competition.

41
00:03:07.327 --> 00:03:09.007
Many rotifers favour defence.

42
00:03:10.327 --> 00:03:12.067
Few rotifers favour fast growth.

43
00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.250
These runs showed prey evolution changing population cycles.

44
00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.650
Predator evolution was not tested.

45
00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:26.630
So this is eco-evolutionary feedback, not shown coevolution.

46
00:03:27.783 --> 00:03:30.863
Pairing partners from different times reveals change.

47
00:03:32.123 --> 00:03:35.183
A trend points to escalation, a wave to tracking.

48
00:03:36.663 --> 00:03:41.263
Coevolution also needs heritable change in both partners, checked with controls.
